## What does "it" mean?

Read this sentence:

> The trophy didn't fit in the suitcase because **it** was too *big*.

What was too big? The trophy. Now change one word:

> The trophy didn't fit in the suitcase because **it** was too *small*.

<At levels="simple">

Now "it" is the suitcase. To work that out, you used "trophy", "suitcase", "fit", and the very
last word, all at once, without even trying.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Now "it" is the suitcase. You didn't have to think about it, but to get there you used "trophy",
"suitcase", "fit", and the very last word, all at once. Sentences like this were collected into a
test for computers, the Winograd Schema Challenge, precisely because they need this kind of
whole-sentence understanding.<Cite id="levesque2012" />

</At>

## Our models so far are short-sighted

The counting model from <ChapterRef to="predicting-by-counting" /> only looked at the one word before the gap. The tiny model from <ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" />
looked at 3 characters. Slide the window below and see how much of the sentence it takes before a
model has everything it needs.

<ContextWindow />

The clue word comes last, but the things it is about come much earlier. A model that sees only the
last few words can tell that something was "too big", but not what.

## Why not just look further back?

<At levels="simple">

The obvious fix is a bigger window. But a counting model needs a place in its table for every
combination of words, and with longer windows almost all of them never appear in any
text.<Cite id="jurafsky-martin-ngrams" /> A neural network shares what it learns between similar
words, but a window of fixed size still has a hard edge: anything just outside it is
invisible.<Cite id="bengio2003" />

What we want is a model that can look at **anything** earlier in the text, and decide for itself
which parts matter.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

The obvious fix is a bigger window. For the counting model, each extra word makes the table vastly
bigger. GPT-2's vocabulary has 50,257 tokens, so there are about 2.5 billion possible pairs of
tokens and about 127 trillion possible triples. Most of them never appear in any training text, so a
table of counts would be almost entirely zeros.<Cite id="jurafsky-martin-ngrams" />

A neural network like the one in <ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" /> handles that better, because similar words get similar
vectors and can share what they learn.<Cite id="bengio2003" /> But a fixed window still has two
problems:

- **It has a hard edge.** Anything one word outside the window is invisible, however important.
- **Every slot is fixed.** The network learns "the word 3 back" and "the word 4 back" as separate
  inputs, while what matters is *which* word is relevant, wherever it is.

What we want is a model that can look at **anything** earlier in the text, and decide for itself
which parts matter for the word it's working on.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The fixed window, written down

A window model in the style of Bengio and colleagues feeds the vectors of the last $n$ tokens into
its first layer side by side:

$$
h = f\!\left(W \, [\,e_{t-1};\, e_{t-2};\, \dots;\, e_{t-n}\,] + b\right)
$$

where $e_{t-k}$ is the vector of the token $k$ places back, the brackets mean "put the lists end
to end", and each slot gets its own columns of $W$.<Cite id="bengio2003" /> So a pattern learned
for the word 3 back says nothing about the word 4 back, and a wider window needs more weights. The
tiny model from <ChapterRef to="learning-from-mistakes" /> is this design: 3 characters of 8
numbers each make 24 inputs to 64 neurons, and every extra character would add another
8 × 64 = 512 weights.

</At>

## The context window

<At levels="simple">

Modern models still have a limit, called the <GlossaryPopover term="context-window">context window</GlossaryPopover>,
but it's much larger. GPT-2 can look at 1,024 tokens at once, and newer models such as Llama 3 at
more than 100 times as many.<Cite id="radford2019" /><Cite id="grattafiori2024" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Modern models still have a limit, called the <GlossaryPopover term="context-window">context window</GlossaryPopover>, but it is much larger.
GPT-2 can look at up to 1,024 tokens at once.<Cite id="radford2019" /> Llama 3, from 2024, can look
at up to 128K (about 128,000), more than 100 times as many.<Cite id="grattafiori2024" />
Within that window, every token can use any earlier token.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

A bigger window isn't free. Attention compares every token with every earlier one, so its work
grows with the square of the window's length (see <ChapterRef to="attention" />):

$$
\frac{(128{,}000)^2}{(1{,}024)^2} = 125^2 = 15{,}625
$$

so a full Llama 3 window needs about 15,600 times as many attention scores per head as a full
GPT-2 window.

Prices don't always follow that work. On Anthropic's price list, Claude Haiku 5.5 charges five
times as much per token, input and output, once a prompt is longer than 100,000 tokens, while its
other recent models charge one rate across a million-token window.<Cite id="anthropic-pricing" />

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Understanding a word often needs words from much earlier in the text.
- Counting tables can't grow to cover long contexts: almost every long combination is unseen.
- A fixed window has a hard edge and treats each position separately.
- Models need a way to pick out the relevant earlier words, wherever they are, within a context
  window (1,024 tokens for GPT-2).

<At levels="deep">

- A fixed window gives each slot its own weights; attention's work grows with the square of the
  window.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** how can a model look back at everything and decide what's relevant?
